From 15818c1989bf5cd0d5e7a3d4182555e3b4d228d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: scholzri <33288574+scholzri@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 13:17:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] zmk: drive USB remote wakeup from the HID send path

zmk_usb_hid_send_report() already asks for a wakeup when the endpoint
status is SUSPEND. Two gaps showed up on hardware (Telink B91):

  - The status is not always SUSPEND when the host is asleep. Some host
    sleep flavors leave the device without a clean suspend indication,
    so the request was never issued and the fork fell back to
    re-presenting the device (a full re-enumeration, ~0.6 s of dead HID
    plus the keystrokes typed into it).
  - A wakeup that does not take needs a fallback, but re-presenting
    immediately defeats the wakeup that may still be in flight.

Ask for the wakeup first and re-present only when it is refused (the
host never armed DEVICE_REMOTE_WAKEUP, so Zephyr answers -EACCES) or
when a wakeup driven moments ago demonstrably did not resume the bus.
Also request one when an interrupt IN write fails while configured: a
host that stopped draining the endpoint after arming remote wakeup is
asleep, whatever the reported status says.

Verified on a Rainy 75 (Telink B91) against a sleeping Linux host: the
keypress resumes the bus with no DETACH, no SET_CONFIGURATION and an
unchanged kernel device number.

Signed-off-by: Richard Scholz <33288574+scholzri@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 app/src/usb_hid.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/app/src/usb_hid.c b/app/src/usb_hid.c
index b4eab790..ac585f31 100644
--- a/app/src/usb_hid.c
+++ b/app/src/usb_hid.c
@@ -186,12 +186,27 @@ static const struct hid_ops ops = {
 
 static int zmk_usb_hid_send_report(const uint8_t *report, size_t len) {
     switch (zmk_usb_get_status()) {
-    case USB_DC_SUSPEND:
+    case USB_DC_SUSPEND: {
         // A keypress while suspended is the user asking for the connection
-        // back: hardware without remote wakeup (B91) re-presents the device
-        // instead (see the dead-bus heartbeat).
-        zmk_usb_user_activity_while_suspended();
-        return usb_wakeup_request();
+        // back. Try true USB remote wakeup first: the driver drives resume
+        // signaling and the host restores the link with no re-enumeration.
+        // Fall back to re-presenting the device (dead-bus heartbeat) when
+        // the request is refused (feature not armed / not supported), or
+        // when a wakeup driven moments ago demonstrably didn't resume the
+        // bus (we are still suspended).
+        static int64_t last_wakeup_req;
+        int64_t now = k_uptime_get();
+        int wake_err = usb_wakeup_request();
+
+        if (wake_err != 0 ||
+            (last_wakeup_req != 0 && (now - last_wakeup_req) < 2000)) {
+            zmk_usb_user_activity_while_suspended();
+        }
+        if (wake_err == 0) {
+            last_wakeup_req = now;
+        }
+        return wake_err;
+    }
     case USB_DC_ERROR:
     case USB_DC_RESET:
     case USB_DC_DISCONNECTED:
@@ -211,6 +226,22 @@ static int zmk_usb_hid_send_report(const uint8_t *report, size_t len) {
         // dark-wake enumerations) fails every write with -EAGAIN.
         zmk_usb_report_send_result(err == 0);
 
+        // A failing write with remote wakeup armed means the host stopped
+        // draining the EP after granting us wake permission: drive resume
+        // signaling. Covers host sleeps the suspend detector misses (some
+        // Linux sleep flavors deliver no clean SUSPEND). No-ops with
+        // -EACCES when the host never armed the feature.
+        if (err) {
+            static int64_t last_wake_pulse;
+            int64_t now = k_uptime_get();
+
+            if (now - last_wake_pulse > 1000) {
+                if (usb_wakeup_request() == 0) {
+                    last_wake_pulse = now;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
         return err;
     }
 }
